<aside><title>The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus</title>

<p>If <m>f(t)</m> is a continuous function and <m>F(t)</m> any differentiable function with <m>F'(t)=f(t)</m>, then <me>\int_a^b f(t)\, dt=F(b)-F(a)=F(t)\Big |_a^b</me>
The notation <m>F(t)\Big |_a^b</m> is just short-hand notation for <m>F(b)-F(a)</m>.</p>

</aside>
  
  
<problem><title>Video review questions</title>
<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />

<p>The <term>indefinite integral</term>, <m>\int f(t)dt</m>, which is obtained from considering the antiderivative of the function <m>f</m>, and the <term>definite integral</term>, <m>\int_{a}^{b} f(t)dt</m>, which is derived from a Riemann sum, are two different mathematical ideas that are related by...</p>

<answer name="ans1a">
  <choice>the limit definition of a derivative</choice>
  <choice>the linear approximation of <m>f</m> at <m>a</m> and <m>b</m></choice>
  <choice credit="1">the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus</choice>
</answer>
</exercise>

<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />
<p>The antiderivative <m>F(t)</m>, or <term>indefinite integral</term> <m>\int t^2 dt</m> of the function <m>f(t) = t^2</m> is...</p>
  <answer name="ans2a">
    <choice credit="1"><m>F(t) = \frac{1}{3} t^3 + C</m></choice>
    <choice><m>F(t) = 2t + C</m></choice>
    <choice><m>F(t) = t^2 + C</m></choice>
  </answer>
<p>We now want to compute the <term>definite integral</term> of this function from <m>a = 0</m> to <m>b = 3</m> using the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus. This means that...</p>
  <answer name="ans2b">
    <choice><m>\int_{3}^{0} t^2 dt = F(0) - F(3)</m></choice> 
    <choice credit="1"><m>\int_{0}^{3} t^2 dt = F(3) - F(0)</m></choice>
    <choice><m>\int_{3}^{0} t^2 dt = F(0) + F(3)</m></choice>
  </answer>

<p>If we now substitute the limits of <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> into the antiderivative you found initially and simplify, we arrive at a final answer of...</p>
  <answer name="ans2c">
    <choice credit="1"><m>9</m></choice>
    <choice><m>-9</m></choice>
    <choice><m>6</m></choice>
  </answer>
</exercise>
</problem>
